# Janus kernel config, longterm track (variants.mk): kernel.org's newest
# longterm release, following that moniker from branch to branch (make
# kernel-config-refresh KERNEL_TRACK=longterm when it moves to a new
# branch). janus_stable_defconfig is the stable track's: the two must say
# the same thing (go test ./hack/kconfig, parity-exceptions.txt).
#
# Janus kernel config. Boots under QEMU (virtio-net + DHCP-assigned
# IP via kernel-builtin IP_PNP, no userspace network tooling needed) with
# a static Go init as PID 1, which supervises janusd -> a real static
# HAProxy binary reachable over the network. Also has squashfs +
# dm-verity support (Phase 3: rootfs/assemble.sh builds a verity-protected
# squashfs rootfs image, now the real boot root= via dm-mod.create=),
# a real GPT A/B disk layout (image/disk/assemble.sh), and ACPI+EFI+
# EFI_STUB for UEFI boot via a Unified Kernel Image (image/uki/assemble.sh).
#
# Regenerate via `make kernel-menuconfig KERNEL_TRACK=<track>` (see
# ../../Makefile), which seeds from this file; or from scratch via
# `make ARCH=x86_64 allnoconfig` + `scripts/kconfig/merge_config.sh -m
# fragment.config` +
# `make ARCH=x86_64 olddefconfig`, where fragment.config is the union of:
#
#   # boot-proof baseline (console, initramfs, devtmpfs, binfmt_elf)
#   CONFIG_64BIT=y CONFIG_PRINTK=y CONFIG_EARLY_PRINTK=y CONFIG_TTY=y
#   CONFIG_SERIAL_8250=y CONFIG_SERIAL_8250_CONSOLE=y CONFIG_PROC_FS=y
#   CONFIG_SYSFS=y CONFIG_DEVTMPFS=y CONFIG_DEVTMPFS_MOUNT=y
#   CONFIG_BINFMT_ELF=y CONFIG_BLK_DEV_INITRD=y CONFIG_RD_GZIP=y
#   CONFIG_MULTIUSER=y CONFIG_TMPFS=y CONFIG_SHMEM=y CONFIG_UNIX98_PTYS=y
#
#   # networking (virtio-net under QEMU/KVM, DHCP autoconfig, AF_UNIX
#   # for haproxy's own stats socket)
#   CONFIG_UNIX=y CONFIG_NET=y CONFIG_INET=y CONFIG_PACKET=y
#   CONFIG_NETDEVICES=y CONFIG_NET_CORE=y CONFIG_PCI=y
#   CONFIG_VIRTIO_MENU=y CONFIG_VIRTIO=y CONFIG_VIRTIO_PCI=y
#   CONFIG_VIRTIO_NET=y CONFIG_IP_PNP=y CONFIG_IP_PNP_DHCP=y
#
#   # squashfs + dm-verity (Phase 3 rootfs image, not yet the boot root=)
#   # - both BLK_DEV_DM and SQUASHFS are nested inside gating menus
#   # (MD, MISC_FILESYSTEMS) that must be enabled first or the fragment
#   # symbols below silently don't apply (merge_config.sh gives no
#   # warning when this happens - verify with a plain grep after merging,
#   # not just a clean merge_config.sh run).
#   CONFIG_MISC_FILESYSTEMS=y CONFIG_SQUASHFS=y CONFIG_SQUASHFS_XZ=y
#   CONFIG_MD=y CONFIG_BLK_DEV_DM=y CONFIG_DM_VERITY=y
#
#   # dm-verity root=, no initramfs (Phase 3 cont'd: rootfs/assemble.sh's
#   # squashfs+verity image mounted directly as the real root via the
#   # dm-mod.create= cmdline parameter, no userspace stage at all) - same
#   # gating-menu trap again: VIRTIO_BLK lives under drivers/block's own
#   # "menuconfig BLK_DEV" (default y, but allnoconfig starts everything
#   # off, so it still has to be listed explicitly).
#   CONFIG_BLK_DEV=y CONFIG_VIRTIO_BLK=y CONFIG_DM_INIT=y
#   # DM_VERITY's own `select CRYPTO_HASH` doesn't pull in an actual
#   # sha256 algorithm - it resolves "sha256" by name through the crypto
#   # API (crypto_alloc_shash), which needs CONFIG_CRYPTO_SHA256, not the
#   # CONFIG_CRYPTO_LIB_SHA256 helper other kernel code already pulled in
#   # (found by a real boot failing with "Cannot initialize hash
#   # function (-2)").
#   CONFIG_CRYPTO_SHA256=y
#
#   # persistent STATE partition (Phase 3 cont'd: /etc/janus/pki
#   # needs to survive a reboot, unlike rootfs/init's tmpfs-backed
#   # ephemeral overlay) - a third virtio-blk drive, pre-formatted at
#   # build time (no mkfs on the target, matching the "no package
#   # manager" rule), mounted directly (no dm-verity: this one is meant
#   # to be written to).
#   CONFIG_EXT4_FS=y
#
#   # UEFI boot + Unified Kernel Image (Phase 3 cont'd: systemd-ukify
#   # assembles the UKI - see image/uki/assemble.sh - which chain-loads
#   # into the kernel's OWN embedded EFI stub via the handover protocol,
#   # so EFI_STUB has to be real in the kernel too, not just present in
#   # systemd's own stub binary). CONFIG_EFI itself needs ACPI - bigger
#   # than it looks, this is the first time ACPI has been enabled at
#   # all (still no real disk/partition boot beyond what Phase 3's own
#   # A/B work added).
#   CONFIG_ACPI=y CONFIG_EFI=y CONFIG_EFI_STUB=y
#
#   # mount the ESP at runtime (Phase 3 cont'd: LifecycleService.
#   # Rollback needs to read/write \EFI\BOOT\BOOTX64.EFI and the
#   # per-slot UKIs image/disk/activate-slot.sh stages alongside it,
#   # from a running node - no mtools there, it's a real kernel mount).
#   # VFAT_FS alone isn't enough - the actual codepage/iocharset tables
#   # FAT_DEFAULT_CODEPAGE=437/FAT_DEFAULT_IOCHARSET=iso8859-1 name are
#   # each their own separate NLS symbol, and mounting failed outright
#   # ("codepage cp437 not found") without them.
#   CONFIG_VFAT_FS=y CONFIG_NLS_CODEPAGE_437=y CONFIG_NLS_ISO8859_1=y
#
#   # Phase 4: kernel self-protection hardening (KSPP-style; a real CIS
#   # benchmark's own kernel-adjacent controls, not just userspace ones -
#   # sysctl-level hardening, applied at boot by rootfs/init since there's
#   # no sysctl(8)/procps on the target, is a separate, later piece). Most
#   # of what CIS/KSPP call out (loadable-module restrictions, /dev/mem,
#   # kexec, hibernation, USB/Firewire/staging drivers) was already true
#   # by construction from allnoconfig - this block is what's left:
#   CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR=y CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR_STRONG=y
#   CONFIG_SLAB_FREELIST_RANDOM=y CONFIG_SLAB_FREELIST_HARDENED=y
#   CONFIG_HARDENED_USERCOPY=y CONFIG_FORTIFY_SOURCE=y
#   CONFIG_INIT_ON_ALLOC_DEFAULT_ON=y CONFIG_INIT_ON_FREE_DEFAULT_ON=y
#   # INIT_ON_FREE zeroes freed kernel memory (defense against use-after-
#   # free/info-leak bugs) at a real, non-trivial cost - more noticeable
#   # than INIT_ON_ALLOC's under allocation-heavy kernel-side workloads
#   # (e.g. high connection/packet churn). Worth remembering as the first
#   # thing to reconsider if a future real-traffic benchmark shows this
#   # project's network fast path regressing - not disabled preemptively
#   # here, since no such benchmark exists yet to say it actually matters
#   # for HAProxy's own (mostly userspace-pool-allocated) hot path.
#   #
#   # CONFIG_SECURITY is the master gate every LSM (SELinux, Yama, ...)
#   # needs - unset until now, so nothing gated behind it (not just Yama)
#   # could ever have been enabled at all, even by accident. Only Yama
#   # (ptrace_scope restriction - relevant here since janusd runs as
#   # root and a compromised instance could otherwise ptrace anything)
#   # is turned on; verified via a real `diff` of the fully-resolved
#   # config, not just a clean merge, that enabling the master gate
#   # didn't silently pull in some *other* LSM along with it - the only
#   # side effects were CONFIG_INTEGRITY=y (an inert framework dependency
#   # with no IMA/EVM policy active, since neither is enabled) and a
#   # cosmetic CONFIG_LSM= default-ordering string listing LSMs that
#   # aren't actually compiled in at all. Yama's own restriction *level*
#   # (kernel.yama.ptrace_scope) isn't a Kconfig knob - rootfs/init sets
#   # it at boot alongside the rest of Phase 4's sysctl hardening.
#   #
#   # CONFIG_STRICT_MODULE_RWX was considered and dropped: it depends on
#   # CONFIG_MODULES, which is - and stays - unset (no loadable module
#   # support compiled in at all is a strictly stronger guarantee than
#   # merely protecting loaded modules' memory permissions would be).
#   CONFIG_SECURITY=y CONFIG_SECURITY_YAMA=y
#   # rootfs/init/main.go's hardenSysctls also sets net.ipv4.tcp_syncookies
#   # (SYN flood protection - directly relevant for a reverse proxy/load
#   # balancer's own exposure) - but that /proc/sys node doesn't exist at
#   # all without CONFIG_SYN_COOKIES, caught by a real boot logging "no
#   # such file or directory" for exactly that one write and nothing
#   # else, not assumed from CONFIG_INET/CONFIG_NET already being on.
#   CONFIG_SYN_COOKIES=y
#
#   # Phase 4 cont'd: SELinux kernel enablement (no policy compiled or
#   # loaded yet - this slice is deliberately just "the kernel can do
#   # SELinux", proven by a real boot showing the LSM initialize, not
#   # "Janus enforces a policy" - see docs/architecture.md for the
#   # actual policy/loading work still open). CONFIG_SECURITY_SELINUX
#   # itself `depends on SECURITY_NETWORK && AUDIT && NET && INET` in
#   # this kernel version - AUDIT wasn't just missing, it was explicitly
#   # `# CONFIG_AUDIT is not set` before this, found by grepping the
#   # real Kconfig dependency line rather than assumed from memory.
#   # AUDIT's own `select`-driven AUDITSYSCALL/FSNOTIFY, and SELinux's
#   # own `select NETWORK_SECMARK`, come along automatically - verified
#   # via a full resolved-config diff, same discipline as every other
#   # symbol in this file, and equally clean: no other LSM (smack/
#   # apparmor/tomoyo) got pulled in, no XFRM/IPSec surface either.
#   CONFIG_AUDIT=y CONFIG_SECURITY_NETWORK=y CONFIG_SECURITY_SELINUX=y
#   # SELINUX_DEVELOP keeps the kernel bootable in permissive mode
#   # (log, don't deny) unless `enforcing=1` is on the cmdline - exactly
#   # what this slice needs, since there's no policy to enforce yet.
#   # SELINUX_BOOTPARAM adds a `selinux=0` escape hatch on the kernel
#   # cmdline - not for production use (the cmdline is baked into the
#   # UKI permanently at build time, there's no boot menu to edit it
#   # from on a running node), but it lets hack/qemu-*-test.sh's plain
#   # `-kernel`/`-append` boots (which don't go through a UKI at all)
#   # disable SELinux for a specific test run without a second kernel
#   # build, the same role CONFIG_SECURITY_SELINUX_DEVELOP's permissive
#   # default already plays for cmdline-less UKI boots.
#   CONFIG_SECURITY_SELINUX_DEVELOP=y CONFIG_SECURITY_SELINUX_BOOTPARAM=y
#   # xattr support on both filesystems that will ever hold a labeled
#   # file: the read-only squashfs rootfs (SQUASHFS_XATTR - mksquashfs
#   # already preserves xattrs set on source files by default, so this
#   # is the only kernel-side piece needed for that half) and the
#   # writable ext4 STATE partition (EXT4_FS_SECURITY - distinct from
#   # EXT4_FS_POSIX_ACL, which is unrelated and still off).
#   CONFIG_SQUASHFS_XATTR=y CONFIG_EXT4_FS_SECURITY=y
#   # "selinux" added to the ordered LSM list (still no-op without a
#   # loaded policy) - inserted in the same relative position the
#   # kernel's own un-customized CONFIG_LSM default uses.
#   CONFIG_LSM="landlock,lockdown,yama,loadpin,safesetid,selinux,ipe,bpf"
#
# Node network configuration (2026-09-30): nodes can have several
# interfaces and 802.1Q VLANs, configured by janusd over rtnetlink.
# `scripts/config --enable VLAN_8021Q` then olddefconfig; the full
# resolved diff is exactly VLAN_8021Q=y plus its GVRP/MVRP options
# staying off (no dynamic VLAN registration protocols).
#   CONFIG_VLAN_8021Q=y
#
# QEMU guest agent extension (2026-10-01): its channel is a virtio-serial
# port. `scripts/config --enable VIRTIO_CONSOLE` then olddefconfig; the
# full resolved diff is exactly VIRTIO_CONSOLE=y plus HVC_DRIVER=y, which
# it selects.
#   CONFIG_VIRTIO_CONSOLE=y
#
# Optional modules (2026-10-01): nftables for the firewall extension -
# nf_tables for inet/ip/ip6/netdev with conntrack, NAT, logging, reject,
# fib, limit/quota/connlimit, hash/numgen, synproxy, and socket + TPROXY
# for HAProxy's transparent proxying; no xtables/iptables. Policy routing
# and ECMP (multiple tables, multipath) for BIRD. `scripts/config
# --enable` each of these, then olddefconfig; the full resolved diff is
# exactly them plus their dependencies (NETFILTER_NETLINK, conntrack's
# SCTP/UDP-Lite protocols and CRC32C, NF_DEFRAG_*, NF_REJECT_*,
# NF_SOCKET_*, NF_TPROXY_*, NETFILTER_SYNPROXY/CONNCOUNT, FIB_RULES,
# NET_INGRESS/EGRESS):
#   CONFIG_NETFILTER=y CONFIG_NETFILTER_ADVANCED=y CONFIG_NETFILTER_INGRESS=y
#   CONFIG_NF_CONNTRACK=y CONFIG_NF_TABLES=y CONFIG_NF_TABLES_INET=y
#   CONFIG_NF_TABLES_NETDEV=y CONFIG_NF_TABLES_IPV4=y CONFIG_NF_TABLES_IPV6=y
#   CONFIG_NFT_CT=y CONFIG_NFT_LIMIT=y CONFIG_NFT_LOG=y CONFIG_NF_LOG_SYSLOG=y
#   CONFIG_NFT_REJECT=y CONFIG_NFT_REJECT_INET=y CONFIG_NF_NAT=y
#   CONFIG_NFT_NAT=y CONFIG_NFT_MASQ=y CONFIG_NFT_REDIR=y
#   CONFIG_NFT_FIB_INET=y CONFIG_NFT_FIB_IPV4=y CONFIG_NFT_FIB_IPV6=y
#   CONFIG_NFT_CONNLIMIT=y CONFIG_NFT_QUOTA=y CONFIG_NFT_NUMGEN=y
#   CONFIG_NFT_HASH=y CONFIG_NFT_SYNPROXY=y CONFIG_NFT_SOCKET=y
#   CONFIG_NFT_TPROXY=y
#   CONFIG_IP_ADVANCED_ROUTER=y CONFIG_IP_MULTIPLE_TABLES=y
#   CONFIG_IP_ROUTE_MULTIPATH=y CONFIG_IPV6_MULTIPLE_TABLES=y
#
# keepalived extension (2026-10-01): its track_file follows a file with
# inotify ("Unable to monitor track files" without it, found by the VRRP
# QEMU test). `scripts/config --enable INOTIFY_USER`, olddefconfig: the
# full resolved diff is exactly INOTIFY_USER=y.
#   CONFIG_INOTIFY_USER=y
#
# bird extension (2026-10-01): IP_MULTICAST (IGMP, for OSPF/RIP/Babel
# groups) and TCP_MD5SIG (BGP session passwords - RFC 2385, what most
# peers ask for). The full resolved diff is those two plus what
# TCP_MD5SIG selects: TCP_SIGPOOL, CRYPTO_MD5, CRYPTO_LIB_MD5.
#   CONFIG_IP_MULTICAST=y
#   CONFIG_TCP_MD5SIG=y
#
# Bare metal (2026-10-01): until now the kernel only knew QEMU's virtio
# devices - and was uniprocessor. Enabled, then checked one by one in the
# resolved config (olddefconfig drops a symbol whose menu is off):
#   CPUs: SMP (NR_CPUS=512), X86_X2APIC (a BIOS can hand over in x2APIC
#     mode - it needs IRQ_REMAP: IOMMU_SUPPORT, INTEL_IOMMU, AMD_IOMMU - or
#     HYPERVISOR_GUEST, with KVM_GUEST/PARAVIRT for kvmclock), NUMA +
#     X86_64_ACPI_NUMA, PCI_MSI (MSI-X, required by most server NICs),
#     X86_MCE (+ _INTEL, _AMD).
#   Disks: ATA, SATA_AHCI, ATA_PIIX, SCSI, BLK_DEV_SD, BLK_DEV_SR (a
#     cloud-init CD-ROM), SCSI_VIRTIO (Proxmox's default controller),
#     BLK_DEV_NVME, MEGARAID_SAS, SCSI_MPT3SAS, SCSI_SMARTPQI,
#     VMWARE_PVSCSI, USB mass storage (USB, XHCI/EHCI, USB_STORAGE,
#     USB_UAS) for an installer stick.
#   NICs (none needs a firmware file): E1000, E1000E, IGB, IGC, IXGBE,
#     I40E, R8169, TIGON3, BNXT, MLX5_CORE(_EN), VMXNET3.
#   Screen: FB, FB_EFI, FRAMEBUFFER_CONSOLE, FONT_8x16 - the console on
#     a monitor, not only on a serial port.
# Side effects reviewed in the full diff: INTEL_IOMMU_DEFAULT_ON (DMA
# remapping on, lazy), CPU_FREQ with schedutil and the Intel/AMD pstate
# drivers, TREE_RCU, I2C (NIC sensors) - nothing unrelated.
#
# prometheus-node-exporter (2026-10-01): what its collectors read, found
# by turning every one on under QEMU (node_scrape_collector_success 0) -
# PSI (/proc/pressure, the default "pressure" collector: it never
# reported anything before), DMIID (/sys/class/dmi/id, the "dmi"
# collector) and NF_CONNTRACK_PROCFS (the conntrack table's statistics).
# `scripts/config --enable`, olddefconfig: the full resolved diff is
# exactly those three (PSI_DEFAULT_DISABLED stays unset).
#   CONFIG_PSI=y CONFIG_DMIID=y CONFIG_NF_CONNTRACK_PROCFS=y
